From 7d176705ce160c700941f9139f916f29fdd3e7ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: scada <scada@noreply.codeberg.org>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:27:36 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 67/80] iio: qcom-smgr: timestamp buffered samples

qcom_smgr_iio_data.timestamp was never written, so every sample pushed
to the buffer carried stale stack contents in the timestamp channel
(Android saw 0). Stamp each report with iio_get_time_ns(), which follows
the device's current_timestamp_clock.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0144VMKxYe33NKa3LLPZNivr
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index 7314ec107357..b34a095d8478 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -292,6 +292,7 @@ static void qcom_smgr_buffering_report_handler(struct qmi_handle *hdl,
 	 */
 	for (j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(ind->samples[0].values); ++j)
 		iio_data.values[j] = ind->samples[0].values[j];
+	iio_data.timestamp = iio_get_time_ns(sensor->iio_dev);
 
 	/*
 	 * SMGR reports sensor data in 32-bit fixed-point values, with 16 bits
